I'll choose J1 because it's an honor and I want to move toward a hand without honors for patterns like All Simples or All Chows.\n\nAfter discarding J1, my hand will be: W7, T4, T5, T6, T7, T8, B3, B6, B7, B8. That's 10 tiles, all simples, with good sequence potential. I can then aim for All Simples (2 points) + All Chows (2 points) + possibly others like Pure Double Chow, etc., to reach 8 points.\n\nBut I also need to consider that All Chows requires four Chows and a pair of suited tiles. My meld is a Chow, and I can potentially form three more Chows from hand: T4-T6, T6-T8, B6-B8. That would use T4, T5, T6, T6, T7, T8, B6, B7, B8. That's 9 tiles, but I have only one T6, so I need another T6 to form two Chows involving T6. Actually, T6 is needed for both T4-T6 and T6-T8, so I would need two T6 tiles. Currently I have only one T6. So to have both Chows, I need to draw another T6. Alternatively, I could form T5-T7 Chow instead of T4-T6, so I don't need two T6. Possibilities are flexible.\n\nGiven the tile efficiency, keeping all T tiles is good.  \n\nSo final decision: discard J1.\n\nReason: Discarding J1 removes an honor tile, allowing the hand to aim for patterns like All Simples and All Chows, and it is less useful for forming sequences compared to the suited tiles. The hand has strong potential for multiple chows in the T and B suits, and J1 does not contribute to that. Keeping all T and B tiles maximizes flexibility for completing melds.\n\nAnswer: Play J1"}
