om unshown:  \n- T4 (2 left), T5 (2), T6 (2), T7 (4), T8 (2).  \n- W8 (4 left).  \n- B7 (2 left \u2014 I have one B7 in hand, meld has B6-B7-B8, so B7 in meld is used, but I could still pair B7 from hand).  \n- F3 (3 left).  \n\nConsidering potential patterns:  \n- All Chows: with two Dots chows + possible Bamboo chows + pair of something (maybe B7). Discarding honors/terminals helps All Simples.  \n- Outside Hand unlikely because I have no terminals/honors in melds/hand except F3.  \n- I should aim for a fast hand: keep flexible sequences.  \n\nDiscarding F3 is safe\u2014no one has called F3 much (history: across played F3 early, prev played none, next none). It\u2019s not a scoring wind. Discarding W8 also safe but W8 might pair. However, keeping W8 doesn\u2019t help sequences much.  \n\nMy hand after discarding F3: W8, T4-5-6-7-8, B7. That\u2019s 7 tiles, can form two Bamboo chows (T4-5-6 and T6-7-8) but they overlap on T6. Actually, with T4-5-6-7-8, I can make two chows: T4-5-6 and T6-7-8, using T6 twice, which is fine if I get an extra T6 or adjust. But currently I have only one of each, so I need draws to complete chows.  \n\nBetter to discard a Bamboo end to keep middle tiles: T4 or T8. T4 has fewer left (2) than T8 (2) same, but T8 is closer to terminal, might be slightly riskier for giving others chow? Others' discards: next player played T8 early; across played T6, T5; previous played T8. So T8 has been discarded; maybe safe to discard T8 again.  \n\nBut to maximize my own draws, keeping T4-5-6-7 is good, discarding T8 leaves T4-5-6-7, which can form T4-5-6 chow and T5-6-7 chow. That\u2019s better because T7 has 4 left.  \n\n**Decision**: Discard T8 to keep T4-5-6-7 sequence and B7 pair chance. Also discard honor F3? But T8 seems more expendable.  \n\nCheck valid actions: Play T8 is allowed.  \n\nReason: Discarding T8 maintains a flexible T4-5-6-7 sequence that can form chows with many draws (T3, T4, T5, T6, T7, T8, T9\u2014actually T4-5-6 needs T4,T5,T6; T5-6-7 needs T5,T6,T7). It also removes a tile that has been discarded before (lower risk). Keeping F3 might allow a pung if I draw two more F3, but that\u2019s less likely and F3 doesn\u2019t score for wind. My hand is more suited-tile oriented, so discarding F3 also good, but I think T8 is slightly better to discard to tighten sequence.  \n\nFinal choice: Play T8.\n\nReason: To optimize chow potential in Bamboo suit while discarding a relatively safe tile that has been seen in discards, and keeping honor F3 temporarily as it might become a pair or be discarded later if not useful.\n\nAnswer: Play T8"}
